/*!
    \page guide-architecture.html
    \title Architecture
    \previouspage {Building and Running}
    \nextpage {Coding Conventions}

    Creature Wars is built like an MMORPG server monolith. One core owns the game world and
    the client only shows it. Right now both live in one process, but they only talk through
    a narrow interface, so the core could move into a server without big changes.

    \section1 Layers

    \table
        \header \li Layer \li Where \li Knows about
        \row \li Game core
             \li \c backend/*.h, \c backend/*.cpp with \c _t types
             \li Plain C++20 and nlohmann/json. No Qt.
        \row \li Qt bridge
             \li \l GameObserver, \l GameBackend and the list models
             \li The core and Qt, but not the QML files.
        \row \li Client
             \li \c qml/
             \li Felgo, QML and the properties and models of \l GameBackend.
    \endtable

    \section1 Threads

    There are two threads. The GUI thread runs QML, Felgo and the bridge. The game thread
    belongs to \l game_t and is the only thread that ever reads or writes the game state.
    The two meet in exactly two places:

    \list
        \li Commands. \c request_* functions of \l game_t take a small lambda, push it into
            a mutex protected queue and wake the game thread.
        \li Events. The game thread calls \l igame_observer_t. \l GameObserver copies the data
            and posts it to the GUI thread with \c Qt::QueuedConnection.
    \endlist

    Nothing else is shared, so the game logic needs no locking at all.

    \section1 Inside the game thread

    \list 1
        \li Sleep until the next scheduled event or creature step, or until a command arrives.
        \li Move queued commands into \l game_event_dispatcher_t.
        \li Move every event from \l game_scheduler_t whose time has come into the dispatcher.
        \li Run the dispatcher, which executes everything in arrival order.
        \li Advance creature movement that is due.
    \endlist

    The game tick is itself a scheduled event that runs once per second of game time and
    reschedules itself. It lets creatures think, attack, regenerate and removes the dead.
    Income, base orders, AI decisions and wildlife spawns are scheduled events too.

    \l game_clock_t sits between the wall clock and the scheduler. Pausing stops game time and
    changing the speed scales it, and scheduled events simply follow.

    \section1 Controllers

    Each race in a match has a controller with its resources, research, order queues and a
    flag that says whether a human plays it. Player input and AI decisions end up in the same
    functions, so both follow the same rules. \l ai_planner_t decides for the computer
    controlled races, with numbers from \c data/ai_profiles.json.

    \section1 Data

    \table
        \header \li File \li Content
        \row \li \c data/creature_types.json \li Creature stats, colours, behaviour, area damage
        \row \li \c data/base_types.json \li Base stats per level, units with training costs, rewards
        \row \li \c data/tech_tree.json \li Base upgrade costs and research paths
        \row \li \c data/economy.json \li Starting resources, income and the income bonus per level
        \row \li \c data/ai_profiles.json \li Easy, normal and hard AI settings
    \endtable

    All files are loaded once when \l GameBackend creates the game. Invalid values throw an
    exception with a message, so mistakes show up right at start.

    \section1 Client structure

    \table
        \header \li File \li Role
        \row \li \c qml/Main.qml \li \c GameWindow with the three scenes and the state machine between them
        \row \li \c qml/scenes/MenuScene.qml \li Race and difficulty selection
        \row \li \c qml/scenes/GameScene.qml \li Map, HUD bars and connections to \l GameBackend
        \row \li \c qml/scenes/GameOverScene.qml \li Result screen
        \row \li \c qml/components/WorldMap.qml \li Tiles, layers, selection, orders and effects
        \row \li \c qml/components/CreatureView.qml \li One creature: sprite, walk, health bar, outlines
        \row \li \c qml/components/BaseView.qml \li One base: tinted art, range, radial menu
    \endtable

    \section1 Toward online play

    To split the game into a server and clients, the plan is to keep \l game_t as it is and
    replace the two meeting points. The observer would serialize events into messages and
    send them to connected clients. The \c request_* calls would come from network messages.
    The QML client would keep its models and simply fill them from the network instead of
    from a local \l GameObserver.
*/
